⍝ APL — Impulse response function from state-space system
⎕IO←0 ⋄ ⎕ML←1
A ← 2 2 ⍴ 0.9 0.1 0.0 0.8    ⍝ transition matrix
B ← 2 1 ⍴ 0.5 0.3             ⍝ shock loading vector
H ← 20                         ⍝ horizons
⍝ IRF: {A^h × B} for h=0,1,...,H-1
irf ← {(A⍣⍵) +.× B} ¨ ⍳ H   ⍝ apply A^h each, using ⍣h
⍝ Stack into H×2 matrix
⊃ irf
)OFF
⎕IO←0
A ← 2 2 ⍴ 0.9 0.1 0.0 0.8    ⍝ transition matrix
B ← 2 1 ⍴ 0.5 0.3             ⍝ shock loading vector
H ← 20                         ⍝ horizons
⍝ IRF: {A^h × B} for h=0,1,...,H-1
irf ← {(A⍣⍵) +.× B} ¨ ⍳ H   ⍝ apply A^h each, using ⍣h
⍝ Stack into H×2 matrix
)OFF
⎕IO←0
A ← 2 2 ⍴ 0.9 0.1 0.0 0.8   ⍝ transition matrix
B ← 0.5 0.3                 ⍝ simplified shock vector (1D is easier to stack)
H ← 20                      ⍝ horizons
⍝ IRF calculation: apply the function (A+.×) ⍵ times to B
⍝ We use {( (A+.×)⍣⍵ ) B} to treat the transition as a repeated function
irf ← { ( (A+.×)⍣⍵ ) B } ¨ ⍳ H
⍝ Use Mix (↑) to turn the vector of vectors into an H×2 matrix
↑ irf
)OFF
⎕IO←0
A ← 2 2 ⍴ 0.9 0.1 0.0 0.8
B ← 0.5 0.3                ⍝ Keep B as a simple vector for easier stacking
H ← 20
⍝ Remove the ∘ binding. Just let A be the left argument.
⍝ (A +.×⍣⍵) B means: Apply (A +.×) to B, ⍵ times.
irf ← { (A +.×⍣⍵) B } ¨ ⍳ H
⎕← '--- IRF Results (First 5 Horizons) ---'
⎕← ↑ 5 ↑ irf
)OFF
